  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Sinusoidal Signals",
    "lesson_core_move": "Express a continuous-time sinusoidal signal in sine or cosine form and read its angular frequency and period from the coefficient of time.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Sinusoidal Signals",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Sinusoidal Signals",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A continuous-time sinusoid is a signal you can write as a sine or cosine with a linear argument in $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\cos(\\omega t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The same template also covers sine, so the two standard forms are the cosine form and the sine form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\sin(\\omega t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The number multiplying $t$ is the angular frequency $\\omega$, not the period. Read that coefficient first.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use $T=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}$ to convert $\\omega$ into a period, and keep the units consistent: radians per second for $\\omega$ and seconds for $T$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega=\\frac{2\\pi}{T}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the base case $x(t)=\\cos(t)$, $\\omega=1$ rad/s and $T=2\\pi$ s, so this is the simplest reference sinusoid.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\cos(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Sinusoidal Signals",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Sine and Cosine Signals",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Recognize that $x(t)=\\cos(3t)$ is a sinusoidal signal written in cosine form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the cosine template from the previous step: a sinusoid is a sine or cosine with a linear time argument.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\cos(3t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This matches the cosine form because the argument $3t$ is linear in $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\cos(\\omega t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $x(t)=\\cos(3t)$ is a sinusoidal signal written in cosine form. Keep the focus on the form; period conversion comes later.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Sinusoidal Signals",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Reading Angular Frequency from a Sinusoid",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=2\\sin(\\frac{5}{3}t)$, identify the angular frequency.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the sinusoid template from the previous step: a sine or cosine with a linear time argument. The angular frequency is the number multiplying $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=2\\sin\\left(\\frac{5}{3}t\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here the argument is $\\frac{5}{3}t$, so the coefficient of $t$ is $\\omega=\\frac{5}{3}$. The amplitude factor $2$ does not affect the angular frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega=\\frac{5}{3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the angular frequency is $\\omega=\\frac{5}{3}$ rad/s. Keep the task at this read-off step; period conversion comes later.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Sinusoidal Signals",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Finding the Period of a Sinusoidal Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=\\cos(\\pi t)$, find the period.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the sinusoid rule from the earlier steps: first read $\\omega$ from the coefficient of $t$, then convert it to period with $T=2\\pi/\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\cos(\\pi t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here the coefficient of $t$ is $\\omega=\\pi$ rad/s. That is the value to substitute into the period formula.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\pi}=2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the period is $T=2$ s. The key move is the conversion from angular frequency to time period.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Sinusoidal Signals",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Writing a Sinusoid from Its Angular Frequency",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Write a cosine signal with angular frequency $4$ rad/s and verify its period.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the given angular frequency. In the simplest construction, use cosine form with unit amplitude and zero phase, and place the number multiplying $t$ inside the cosine.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\cos(4t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This signal has angular frequency $\\omega=4$ rad/s because the coefficient of $t$ is $4$. The period comes from the same conversion rule used earlier.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T=\\frac{2\\pi}{4}=\\frac{\\pi}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So one valid answer is $x(t)=\\cos(4t)$, and its period is $T=\\pi/2$ s. The frequency is set by the time coefficient, not by the amplitude.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
